WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.270 --> 00:00:01.330
MARK MIRCHANDANI:
On this episode,

00:00:01.330 --> 00:00:02.760
we talk to doc.ai
about how they're

00:00:02.760 --> 00:00:04.200
bringing the power
to the people when

00:00:04.200 --> 00:00:05.408
it comes to medical research.

00:00:09.290 --> 00:00:11.070
Akshay, thank you so
much for joining us.

00:00:11.070 --> 00:00:13.490
Can you tell us a little bit
more about what doc.ai is?

00:00:13.490 --> 00:00:15.840
AKSHAY SHARMA: First of all,
thank you for having me.

00:00:15.840 --> 00:00:17.270
Happy to share what doc.ai is.

00:00:17.270 --> 00:00:19.400
So doc.ai is a
digital health company

00:00:19.400 --> 00:00:22.670
designed for a new generation
of medical research.

00:00:22.670 --> 00:00:25.010
And what it really
means is that today,

00:00:25.010 --> 00:00:28.730
the world of medical research is
very localized to your doctors

00:00:28.730 --> 00:00:32.509
deciding where you, the
patient, should be ushered

00:00:32.509 --> 00:00:35.590
into for clinical trials
or what type of drugs

00:00:35.590 --> 00:00:36.960
that you should be on.

00:00:36.960 --> 00:00:41.450
So what we are seeing is that
patients and general users

00:00:41.450 --> 00:00:45.230
aren't involved in the
world of medical research.

00:00:45.230 --> 00:00:47.760
They don't have agency
to make decisions.

00:00:47.760 --> 00:00:51.620
So with doc.ai, we are
building a bunch of ecosystems

00:00:51.620 --> 00:00:55.260
of tools, including a doc.ai
companion medical research app,

00:00:55.260 --> 00:00:57.260
but also, other tools
and technologies

00:00:57.260 --> 00:00:59.330
using artificial
intelligence that

00:00:59.330 --> 00:01:02.930
allows patients to take
ownership of their own data

00:01:02.930 --> 00:01:05.750
and also be able to decide
what type of medical research

00:01:05.750 --> 00:01:08.787
that they want to participate
in, all through the mobile app.

00:01:08.787 --> 00:01:10.370
MARK MIRCHANDANI:
So you mentioned AI.

00:01:10.370 --> 00:01:12.260
What aspect of AI
are you bringing

00:01:12.260 --> 00:01:15.090
to the users who are using
the doc.ai's platform?

00:01:15.090 --> 00:01:16.632
AKSHAY SHARMA: It's
a great question.

00:01:16.632 --> 00:01:19.790
So I think what we care
about is, how can users

00:01:19.790 --> 00:01:23.030
collect medical signals
and medical information

00:01:23.030 --> 00:01:26.570
and capture all of this
data on their mobile phone,

00:01:26.570 --> 00:01:28.010
using their mobile phone?

00:01:28.010 --> 00:01:30.230
So what that means
is that we can

00:01:30.230 --> 00:01:33.570
use different components of
a phone like, for example,

00:01:33.570 --> 00:01:36.590
your camera, but
also your voice, also

00:01:36.590 --> 00:01:38.060
things like chat bots.

00:01:38.060 --> 00:01:41.210
So we invest in a lot of
tools and technologies

00:01:41.210 --> 00:01:46.080
that help us roll out different
AI components for data capture.

00:01:46.080 --> 00:01:48.020
We are also
investing quite a bit

00:01:48.020 --> 00:01:50.840
in privacy and
security-preserving tools

00:01:50.840 --> 00:01:53.630
and technologies, like
Federated Learning,

00:01:53.630 --> 00:01:55.160
along with
differential privacy so

00:01:55.160 --> 00:01:57.630
that we can keep the
data on the phone

00:01:57.630 --> 00:01:59.900
but still use the
data on the phone

00:01:59.900 --> 00:02:03.560
to train models that can
give a global perspective

00:02:03.560 --> 00:02:05.960
of the population without
actually ever sending

00:02:05.960 --> 00:02:06.835
the data to a server.

00:02:06.835 --> 00:02:09.043
MARK MIRCHANDANI: So that's
a lot of different pieces

00:02:09.043 --> 00:02:10.830
that you're covering
as a company.

00:02:10.830 --> 00:02:13.275
How does this relate to
health care as an industry?

00:02:13.275 --> 00:02:14.150
AKSHAY SHARMA: Right.

00:02:14.150 --> 00:02:16.760
So I think what we see
with health care is

00:02:16.760 --> 00:02:20.720
that, without members and
participants being part

00:02:20.720 --> 00:02:22.970
of the health care,
we are not going

00:02:22.970 --> 00:02:26.570
to get enough information
about the progression

00:02:26.570 --> 00:02:29.390
of different diseases or we
are not going to make progress

00:02:29.390 --> 00:02:30.770
in terms of research.

00:02:30.770 --> 00:02:33.230
So an important
aspect of health care

00:02:33.230 --> 00:02:37.790
is, how do we get patients and
users involved in research?

00:02:37.790 --> 00:02:41.160
So one aspect of this
is data collection.

00:02:41.160 --> 00:02:44.030
So with the doc.ai medical
app, what we have built

00:02:44.030 --> 00:02:48.170
is a multiomics or polyomics
way of collecting data.

00:02:48.170 --> 00:02:51.140
So examples of that is we could
collect your genomics data

00:02:51.140 --> 00:02:52.920
and keep the data on the phone.

00:02:52.920 --> 00:02:55.970
We can collect your medical
records, your pharmacy records,

00:02:55.970 --> 00:02:58.650
your medication pill
bottles, your lab records,

00:02:58.650 --> 00:03:01.580
but also newer types of
information, like how

00:03:01.580 --> 00:03:04.570
about your location and how
does that impact your health?

00:03:04.570 --> 00:03:06.060
We call it exposomics.

00:03:06.060 --> 00:03:08.990
So all we need is
your GPS location.

00:03:08.990 --> 00:03:11.177
We can keep that on the
phone for you if you want

00:03:11.177 --> 00:03:13.010
and still be able to
understand your health.

00:03:13.010 --> 00:03:16.490
So we invest quite a bit
in different and new types

00:03:16.490 --> 00:03:20.420
of data collection through
consumer remediation.

00:03:20.420 --> 00:03:22.220
On the other side,
what we want to do

00:03:22.220 --> 00:03:24.380
is be able to get
all of this data

00:03:24.380 --> 00:03:27.830
through the right consent
and opt-in and correlate

00:03:27.830 --> 00:03:30.960
information across
different types of data.

00:03:30.960 --> 00:03:33.740
So we call it the polyomics
data combination problem.

00:03:33.740 --> 00:03:35.900
So be able to understand,
how does your genomics

00:03:35.900 --> 00:03:39.420
information influence
based on where you live?

00:03:39.420 --> 00:03:43.310
So I think this will change
the way it influences

00:03:43.310 --> 00:03:45.800
health care for other
players in the ecosystem

00:03:45.800 --> 00:03:49.580
by having wider adoption
of participants and members

00:03:49.580 --> 00:03:52.605
and patients being more
involved in research.

00:03:52.605 --> 00:03:54.980
MARK MIRCHANDANI: Stay tuned
for more great conversations

00:03:54.980 --> 00:03:55.910
with doc.ai.

